# Generates browns-restless-dust.html from template.html, mechanism.js (the single source of truth) and the embedded fonts,
# then verifies byte for byte that the embedded mechanism is the file calibration.js ran against.
import base64, sys, os
os.chdir(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
mech = open('mechanism.js', encoding='utf-8').read()
font = lambda f: base64.b64encode(open('fonts/' + f, 'rb').read()).decode()
about = """
<h3>The game in one line</h3>
<p>Dust is dancing in a drop of water under Robert Brown's lens, and you have seven days and his own bench to make it lie still. You cannot. That is the paper.</p>
<h3>What the paper reports</h3>
<p>In the summer of 1827 Brown, keeper of the botanical collections at the British Museum, was looking at pollen to settle how it fertilises a flower. In water, the particles inside the pollen of <i>Clarckia pulchella</i> (oblong, about a four-thousandth to a five-thousandth of an inch long) moved: changing place, turning, even changing shape; and smaller round specks, which he called molecules, about a twenty-thousandth to a thirty-thousandth of an inch across, moved faster still. He satisfied himself that the motion was not currents in the water and not evaporation, but belonged to the particle. Then he went looking for where it stopped. It did not stop: every plant; plants dried twenty years and a hundred; every part of a plant when bruised; animal tissue; gum, coal, the soot of London; fossil wood, window glass, granite (one specimen a fragment of the Sphinx), lava, obsidian, pumice, meteorites, manganese, nickel, bismuth, antimony, arsenic; wood, linen, paper, cotton, wool, silk, hair and muscle after burning in a candle or a blowpipe, as evidently in motion as before. Short fibrils of two or three molecules moved as vividly as a molecule; four or five, less; longer ones lay at rest. He could get nothing, in 1827, from oil, resin, wax or sulphur, nor from anything that dissolves in water. He thought for a while that the molecules were the elementary particles of organic matter, Buffon's idea, and expected to find them only in organic bodies; the window glass ended that.</p>
<p>In 1829 he added a supplement. People had said he claimed the molecules were animated; he had not. People had offered causes: currents, evaporation, attraction and repulsion between the particles, capillary and hygrometric action, air bubbles. He answered them with one experiment: shake a little of the water into almond oil, and drops of water from a fiftieth to a two-thousandth of an inch across are sealed, do not evaporate, and last more than an hour; the motion in them is undiminished; some hold a single particle, which moves; and the currents that run from centre to rim in an open drop (slow at first, then very rapid, until they overcome the proper motion and the drop is gone within a minute) do not occur. Turned round, minute oil droplets on the surface of a water drop lie at rest while the particles inside move. He ended with motions “for which I am unable to account”, and a list of earlier observers who had seen such motion and taken it for life.</p>
<h3>Mechanism: a toy</h3>
<p><b>This game demonstrates</b> that, under its own assumptions, nothing you do to the matter (stone, glass, a meteorite, a moss dead a century, burnt hair, soot), to the drop (sealed in oil for an hour, shrunk to one speck, turned inside out) or to the company the specks keep changes the dance; that the open drop's streams come after the dance and end with the water; and that the one thing that changes it is how small the specks are.</p>
<p><b>It assumes</b> the toy. A dance whose vivacity depends on a speck's size alone, one for a molecule, less for the larger pollen particles, nothing for a grain coarser than anything Brown reports moving (the ordering and the rest limit are his; the curve between them is ours). Currents in an open drop that grow from nothing to very rapid over the drop's minute (his words; our curve). A bench of fourteen looks, each one thing Brown did, each setting aside the suspects his own sentences say it sets aside; five suspects, from his 1829 list and his own first belief; seven days, which are ours and make the order a decision. Speck sizes on the plate are exaggerated, as every drawing of a drop must. Every number sits in the config block at the top of the source with the page it came from; the paper gives them in words, read from a scan of typeset text.</p>
<p><b>The paper establishes</b> the observations as reported: the sizes, the substances, the ages, the burning, the fibrils, the sealed drop, the single particle, the inverted experiment; and the argument from them, that the motion is not life, not currents, not evaporation, not mutual attraction, not the surface. It establishes no cause and claims none. The game's “clue” (size) is Brown's own ordering, not an explanation, and the game offers none inside its play. What explained it later is on the verdict screen, after a rule, marked as later.</p>
<h3>What is cut</h3>
<p>The purpose Brown started with (tracing the pollen's particles through the style to the ovulum) and his disagreement with Brongniart about it; the plant families (Onagrariae, Asclepiadeae, Orchideae, Gramineae, the mosses, Equisetum); the change of form of the Clarckia particles, shown here as turning only; the “primary combinations” hypothesis he advanced in 1827 and withdrew in 1829; his doubt about whether the molecules are all one size (they are drawn one size); the priority discussion (Leeuwenhoek, Gray, Needham, Buffon, Gleichen, Drummond, Bywater); most of the list of substances, three of which are on the bench. In 1827 sulphur, resin and wax yielded nothing; the bench uses that, and the line on screen says 1829 got them. The Sphinx on the bench was a fragment of its granite, examined as one of granite's minerals.</p>
<h3>The paper</h3>
<p>Robert Brown, <i>A brief account of microscopical observations made in the months of June, July and August, 1827, on the particles contained in the pollen of plants; and on the general existence of active molecules in organic and inorganic bodies</i>. Privately printed, London, dated 30 July 1828 (“Not published”); reprinted in the <i>Edinburgh New Philosophical Journal</i> 5 (1828): 358–371 and the <i>Philosophical Magazine</i> 4 (1828): 161–173. With <i>Additional remarks on active molecules</i>, dated 28 July 1829 (<i>Edinburgh Journal of Science</i> 1 (1829): 314–319). Both as reprinted in <i>The miscellaneous botanical works of Robert Brown</i>, vol. 1, ed. J. J. Bennett (London: R. Hardwicke, 1866), pp. 463–486, whose page numbers the source cites. No DOI. The 1866 reprint is scanned at the Biodiversity Heritage Library; the pamphlet is reprinted in many anthologies.</p>
<p><b>Game built from this paper; the paper is the authority where the two disagree.</b> Font: IM Fell English and IM Fell English SC (SIL Open Font License). The only randomness is where the specks sit and how they tremble; it changes nothing that the game reads.</p>
"""
t = open('template.html', encoding='utf-8').read()
keys = ['/*__MECHANISM__*/', '/*__ABOUT__*/', '/*__FONT_FELL__*/', '/*__FONT_FELL_I__*/', '/*__FONT_FELL_SC__*/']
for k in keys:
    assert t.count(k) == 1, k
out = (t.replace('/*__FONT_FELL__*/', font('fell.woff2')).replace('/*__FONT_FELL_I__*/', font('fell-italic.woff2'))
        .replace('/*__FONT_FELL_SC__*/', font('fell-sc.woff2')).replace('/*__ABOUT__*/', about).replace('/*__MECHANISM__*/', mech))
name = 'browns-restless-dust.html'
open(name, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(out)
built = open(name, encoding='utf-8').read()
if mech not in built:
    sys.exit('BUILD FAILED: embedded mechanism differs from mechanism.js')
print(f'built {name}: {len(built)} bytes; mechanism embedded verbatim ({len(mech)} bytes)')
